    "created_at": "2026-08-28T18:05:07Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-08-28T18:05:07Z",
    "body": "A related issue around `window.print()` being blocking that I'm having a lot of trouble with is the following:\n\nIn a web application that must not block (as it could eg. hinder warning audio from playing), using `window.print()` normally is not really an option. But at the same time user expectation is that they have access to individual print buttons inside the application that print a singular part of the application. As such, we have to jump through hoops to make an HTML text copy (extracting out CSS styles as well) of the user-selected part, send that HTML string over to a printing WebWorker, which then draws it into a canvas, extracts out the image blob, turns that into a data URL, and finally that gets sent over to the UI thread where it is put into a sandboxed iframe and the `iframe.contentWindow.print()` is called on it to get a non-blocking print dialog.\n\nNow, this works fine up until WebComponents come along: we cannot simply copy out the HTML and CSS styles of a WebComponent and print it on a WebWorker - the WebWorker does not have that WebComponent defined and it will therefore not know how to draw it. This is kind of blocking the usage of WebComponents for us, while we'd quite like to use them more and more.\n\nNow - an asynchrony in `beforeprint` would not fix the issue that we're having, I expect, but asynchronous printing in general is kind of where our thoughts are drifting into.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
